.TH HYPER-LOADER 1 "2026" "hyper-loader 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hyper-loader \- a native wlr-layer-shell overlay for masking long-running commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hyper-loader
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hyper-loader
draws a fullscreen, click-through overlay on every connected output of a
wlroots-based Wayland compositor (Hyprland, Sway, river, Wayfire, labwc,
\&...) using the
.B zwlr_layer_shell_v1
protocol. It is meant to be shown for the few seconds a long-running command
takes \- a
.BR "nixos-rebuild switch --specialisation" ,
an
.BR ansible-playbook ,
a deploy script \- so the transition feels deliberate instead of the
desktop freezing mid-frame.
.PP
The overlay has no keyboard interactivity and reserves no screen space
(\fIexclusive_zone = \-1\fR), so it never steals focus or shifts other
windows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-accent " " \fIHEX\fR
Primary accent color (hex, e.g. \fB7ed4c8\fR) used for the grid, corners,
pulse rings and the digit counter. Default: \fB7ed4c8\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-accent2 " " \fIHEX\fR
Secondary accent color for grid/corner/pulse decorations. Defaults to
\fB\-\-accent\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-fade\-ms " " \fIMS\fR
Fade-in duration in milliseconds for the overlay decorations. Default:
\fB300\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-gif " " \fIPATH\fR
GIF to loop, centered on screen.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-counter
Disable the scrolling digit-counter overlay.
.TP
.B \-\-glitch
Enable a scanline + glitch flicker effect over the background.
.TP
.BR \-\-overlay\-alpha " " \fI0.0\-1.0\fR
Darkness of the overlay drawn over the blurred screenshot background.
Values outside \fB0.0\-1.0\fR are clamped. Default: \fB0.55\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a TOML config file. See
.B CONFIG FILE
below. CLI flags always win over the config file.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Print help and exit.
.SH CONFIG FILE
In addition to CLI flags,
.B hyper-loader
reads a TOML config file, checked in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
the path given via \fB\-\-config\fR, if any (an error is printed, but startup
still proceeds with defaults, if that specific path doesn't exist)
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hyper-loader/config.toml\fR
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise \fI~/.config/hyper-loader/config.toml\fR (silently skipped if
absent)
.PP
Every CLI flag has a matching config key of the same name (underscores),
e.g.:
.PP
.nf
.RS
accent = "7ed4c8"
accent2 = "b072d1"
fade_ms = 300
gif = "/home/me/.config/hyper-loader/loading.gif"
no_counter = false
glitch = false
overlay_alpha = 0.55
.RE
.fi
.PP
A CLI flag always overrides the same key from the config file.
.SH BACKGROUND SCREENSHOT
.B hyper-loader
polls for a PNG at \fI/tmp/hyper-loader-bg.png\fR. If present when it
starts, it is loaded, box-blurred, and cross-faded in as the backdrop, then
the file is deleted. Typical usage is to run
.B grim
right before launching:
.PP
.nf
.RS
grim /tmp/hyper-loader-bg.png
hyper-loader --accent 7ed4c8 &
LOADER_PID=$!
# wait for /tmp/hyper-loader-ready, run the real command, then:
kill "$LOADER_PID"
.RE
.fi
.SH READINESS SIGNAL
Once at least one output's layer surface is configured (actually visible),
.B hyper-loader
writes \fI/tmp/hyper-loader-ready\fR. Callers spawning it in the background
should poll for that file before starting the real work, to avoid a visible
flash of the plain desktop.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hyper-loader
runs until killed (e.g. \fBSIGTERM\fR/\fBSIGINT\fR from the caller) or every
output it was showing on disappears.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation, examples, and the known single-vs-multi-output history:
.br
https://github.com/sgomezsal/hyper-loader
.SH AUTHOR
sgomezsal
